WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.740
A young male white-crowned sparrow never hears an adult sing.

2
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.080
As an adult, its song is simple and abnormal.

3
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.460
Hearing the right song later does little to fix it.

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.460
The same experience can have a lasting effect at one age and little effect at

5
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.300
another. Genes and experience shape when learning can happen.

6
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.200
Experience can even help close the window.

7
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.960
Timing matters because missing early input can leave lasting deficits in song and

8
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.620
sight, and late language access affects later proficiency.

9
00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:39.813
Genes build the circuit that can learn and set roughly when it can learn.

10
00:00:40.713 --> 00:00:42.353
Experience supplies what is learned.

11
00:00:43.253 --> 00:00:48.493
In zebra finches, birds raised without a tutor can still copy new song after birds

12
00:00:48.493 --> 00:00:50.013
raised with a tutor have stopped.

13
00:00:50.873 --> 00:00:53.453
That suggests hearing a tutor helps close the window.

14
00:00:54.413 --> 00:00:55.733
Age alone does not set it.

15
00:00:56.617 --> 00:00:58.377
What happens inside that window?

16
00:00:59.317 --> 00:01:00.517
Song learning has two phases.

17
00:01:01.437 --> 00:01:04.497
A young bird listens to tutor and memorizes the song.

18
00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:08.537
Then it practices, shaping its own sounds to match the memory.

19
00:01:09.417 --> 00:01:12.377
In white-crowned sparrows, these phases can be months apart.

20
00:01:13.197 --> 00:01:16.137
A sparrow deafened between them sings a very poor song.

21
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:18.797
It must hear itself to correct its voice.

22
00:01:19.697 --> 00:01:23.917
The brain has a motor pathway from HVC through RA to the brainstem.

23
00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:28.977
Another pathway, through area X and LMAN, matters while song is learned.

24
00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:32.707
Sight has different windows for different abilities.

25
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:37.107
Infants born with dense cataracts show what visual input does.

26
00:01:38.007 --> 00:01:42.227
Right after treatment, infants' acuity was no better than a newborn's, whatever

27
00:01:42.227 --> 00:01:47.587
their age. Some improvement appeared after as little as an hour of visual input.

28
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:50.187
Patterned input drives improvement.

29
00:01:51.007 --> 00:01:55.427
Yet fine acuity and processing the spacing of facial features showed lasting

30
00:01:55.427 --> 00:01:57.607
deficits after early deprivation.

31
00:01:57.607 --> 00:02:01.687
Early input can matter even before an ability appears.

32
00:02:02.607 --> 00:02:04.707
Improvement and lasting loss can coexist.

33
00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:09.067
For language, ask first about access to a first language.

34
00:02:10.007 --> 00:02:13.767
Some deaf children of hearing parents first meet a signed language late.

35
00:02:14.807 --> 00:02:16.747
Delayed access affects later proficiency.

36
00:02:17.847 --> 00:02:22.207
These children mainly lacked an accessible language, rather than the whole range of

37
00:02:22.207 --> 00:02:23.607
care and social contact.

38
00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.590
Learning a second language asks a different question.

39
00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.770
These learners already know a first language.

40
00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:35.210
Researchers compared English grammar scores with age of arrival.

41
00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.370
The youngest arrivals scored like native speakers.

42
00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.250
Scores fell with age up to about puberty.

43
00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.650
Later arrivals scored lower and varied widely.

44
00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:46.730
The age effect is clear.

45
00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:52.990
Its cause remains debated: changes in plasticity, an established first language, or

46
00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:54.070
differences in input.

47
00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:55.050
. . . .

48
00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:59.310
Across these cases, ask what input arrives, at what age, and what lasts.

49
00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.350
Experience shapes learning, and can help shape the window itself.
